  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946663",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-48965",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f1d07a6d-3994-5620-a756-ee6e2c0a8de6",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of the Product or Quotient of a Composition of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=-1,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=-2,\\,g(2)=-5,\\,{g}^{′}(2)=1\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)=\\frac{5f(x)+4}{g(x)+6},$ find the value of ${h}^{′}(2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{v{u}^{′}-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5f(x)+4$ and $v=g(x)+6.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{v{u}^{′}-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(g(x)+6)\\cdot 5{f}^{′}(x)-(5f(x)+4)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{f}^{′}(x)(g(x)+6)-{g}^{′}(x)(5f(x)+4)}{(g(x)+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5{f}^{′}(2)(g(2)+6)-{g}^{′}(2)(5f(2)+4)}{(g(2)+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-5+6)-1\\cdot (5\\cdot (-1)+4)}{(-5+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot 1-(-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-9",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-5",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "8",
              "option": "2",
