  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946940",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17237",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score",
      "problem": "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma =10.5$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=35.4$ and $z$-score $z=-1.1.$ Find the mean of the data set.",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=35.4,$ $z=-1.1,$ and $\\sigma =10.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 35.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}+(-1.1)\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ 35.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}-11.55 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =46.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
