  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946940",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-9785",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782",
      "knowledge_point": "Z-Score: Word Problems",
      "problem": "The mean weight of a group of newborn children is $3.2kg$ with a standard deviation of $350g.$\nWhat is the $z$-score that corresponds to a weight of $3kg?$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to convert $350g=0.350kg$\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3.2}{0.350} \\\\  & =-0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places.",
