  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947039",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-75924",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}=4.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x} & =4 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}) & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{x}-x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{x} & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{\\cancel{x}}-\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{x}} & =4x \\\\ x+7-(1-x) & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7-(1-x) & =4x \\\\ 2x+6 & =4x \\\\ 4x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=3\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=3.$",
