  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947039",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-75925",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$",
